{
 "title": "Excel believes in a day that never happened",
 "captions": [
  {
   "text": "Excel thinks February 29th, 1900 was a !real day.!",
   "say": "Excel thinks February twenty-ninth, nineteen hundred, was a real day.",
   "hide": true
  },
  {
   "text": "It wasn't. 1900 was !not! a leap year.",
   "say": "It wasn't. Nineteen hundred was not a leap year."
  },
  {
   "text": "Century years skip leap day unless they divide by *400.*",
   "say": "Century years skip leap day unless they divide by four hundred."
  },
  {
   "text": "Type it into Excel, and it's !accepted! as a date."
  },
  {
   "text": "Why? In the eighties, the top spreadsheet was *Lotus 1-2-3,*",
   "say": "Why? In the eighties, the top spreadsheet was Lotus one-two-three,"
  },
  {
   "text": "and it treated 1900 as a !leap year.!",
   "say": "and it treated nineteen hundred as a leap year."
  },
  {
   "text": "Microsoft copied that, so Lotus files would open *correctly.*"
  },
  {
   "text": "Fixing it now would shift almost every date in existing spreadsheets by !one day.!"
  },
  {
   "text": "So Excel still keeps a day that never existed, *on purpose.*"
  }
 ],
